Title: Report why the D3D9 renderer failed instead of guessing at it
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

Two things that together make a D3D9 failure unreadable, found while working out
why the GPU process will not start on XP. The D3D9 backend is restored by
patches/chromium/third_party/angle/0003..0005, so this sits on top of those.

Renderer9::initialize retries GetDeviceCaps ten times at 100 ms apart, because a
driver can answer D3DERR_NOTAVAILABLE while it is still coming up. A machine
with no Direct3D-capable display driver answers that way every time, and when
the budget runs out the loop simply ends: nothing checks the result. mDeviceCaps
is a plain member that nothing has assigned, so the shader-model and StretchRect
checks immediately below read uninitialized memory, and the renderer is
rejected with "Renderer does not support PS 2.0, aborting!" no matter what the
hardware can actually do. Reading uninitialized memory is a bug on its own; the
misdirection is what costs time. Checking the result turns it into "Failed to
get device caps after 10 attempts, HRESULT: 0x8876086A", which says what
happened.

The second half is that none of those messages were reaching us anyway.
CreateRendererD3D logs each renderer's failure with ANGLE's ERR(), which on
Windows is an fprintf to the stderr of the process ANGLE is linked into -- the
GPU process, whose stderr is not where the console is. What does come back is
the egl::Error, which Chromium surfaces through its EGL_KHR_debug callback, and
that only said "No available renderers" -- the one thing already obvious from
eglInitialize having failed. The reasons now travel with the error as well as
going to ERR(), so a host in another process can see them.

Both are upstream-shaped fixes rather than anything XP-specific, and worth
sending to ANGLE.

diff --git a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/d3d/DisplayD3D.cpp b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/d3d/DisplayD3D.cpp
--- a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/d3d/DisplayD3D.cpp
+++ b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/d3d/DisplayD3D.cpp
@@ -123,6 +123,8 @@
         UNIMPLEMENTED();
     }
 
+    std::string rendererFailures;
+
     for (size_t i = 0; i < rendererCreationFunctions.size(); i++)
     {
         RendererD3D *renderer = rendererCreationFunctions[i](display);
@@ -155,8 +157,24 @@
         // Failed to create the renderer, try the next
         SafeDelete(renderer);
         ERR() << "Failed to create D3D renderer: " << result.getMessage();
+
+        // Carry the reasons out with the error as well as logging them. ERR() goes to
+        // this process's stderr, which is not where a host embedding ANGLE in a child
+        // process is looking; the returned error reaches whatever the host installed as
+        // an EGL_KHR_debug callback. Without this the host is told only that every
+        // renderer failed, which is the one thing it could already see.
+        if (!rendererFailures.empty())
+        {
+            rendererFailures += "; ";
+        }
+        rendererFailures += result.getMessage();
     }
 
+    if (!rendererFailures.empty())
+    {
+        return egl::Error(EGL_NOT_INITIALIZED,
+                          "No available renderers. " + rendererFailures);
+    }
     return egl::Error(EGL_NOT_INITIALIZED, "No available renderers.");
 }
 
diff --git a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/d3d/d3d9/Renderer9.cpp b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/d3d/d3d9/Renderer9.cpp
--- a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/d3d/d3d9/Renderer9.cpp
+++ b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/d3d/d3d9/Renderer9.cpp
@@ -246,7 +246,10 @@
         //  corresponds to
     }
 
-    HRESULT result;
+    // Initialized to a failure, not S_OK: the check after the caps loop below treats
+    // this as "no caps were obtained", so the safe starting value is the one that says
+    // nothing has succeeded yet.
+    HRESULT result = E_FAIL;
 
     // Give up on getting device caps after about one second.
     {
@@ -271,6 +274,19 @@
                 return egl::Error(EGL_NOT_INITIALIZED, D3D9_INIT_OTHER_ERROR, err.str());
             }
         }
+
+        // The retry budget can run out with result still D3DERR_NOTAVAILABLE, which is
+        // what a machine with no Direct3D-capable display driver returns every time.
+        // Falling through leaves mDeviceCaps uninitialized -- it is a plain member that
+        // nothing above assigns -- so the PS version and StretchRect checks below then
+        // test whatever it happens to hold, and the renderer is usually rejected with
+        // "does not support PS 2.0" regardless of what the hardware can do.
+        if (FAILED(result))
+        {
+            std::ostringstream err;
+            err << "Failed to get device caps after 10 attempts, " << gl::FmtHR(result);
+            return egl::Error(EGL_NOT_INITIALIZED, D3D9_INIT_OTHER_ERROR, err.str());
+        }
     }
 
 #if ANGLE_SUPPORT_SHADER_MODEL_2
